---
import ChargingStudy from "../../../components/studies/suns/ChargingStudy.svelte";
import ExposureEconomy from "../../../components/studies/suns/ExposureEconomy.astro";
import SunHudPreview from "../../../components/studies/suns/SunHudPreview.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "suns-2026-11-07");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A star in the fiction, an encounter in play</h2>
    <p>
      A sun must feel like a star that belongs in this universe, and behave in
      ways a pilot can learn. Real stars are the modelling reference; the game’s
      fiction and playability determine the useful granularity. The study will
      define which stellar properties matter, what the fiction allows ships to
      do around them, and how those choices become gameplay character,
      appearance and feedback.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central tension is between a coherent stellar model and an encounter
      that remains readable and enjoyable at the game’s scale. Size, colour,
      heat, gravity and stellar activity are candidate dimensions to
      investigate, not a commitment to simulate every phenomenon. We need to
      decide which relationships to preserve, simplify or deliberately depart
      from, and why. The fiction must also explain the ship’s side of the
      encounter: what it can withstand, exploit and sense. Existing sun types
      and mechanics are material to examine, not the definition we must
      preserve.
    </p>
    <p>
      A close pass provides a concrete test: why approach, what changes as the
      ship draws nearer, and how can the pilot judge an escape? The outcome
      should connect each sun’s identity to expected behaviour and the cues that
      communicate it. Small visual, UX and HUD adjustments belong here when they
      support that model. A broader flight redesign remains outside this cycle.
      Retained definitions, examples and deliberate fictional allowances should
      make the eventual Suns article straightforward to write, distinguishing
      intended behaviour from what the game currently does.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="exploration">
    <h2>02 / Stellar properties become pilot decisions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>02.1 / Stellar model and settled decisions</summary>
      <h3 id="stellar-model">
        02.1 / Preserve relationships, compress the scale
      </h3>
      <p>
        Adopted model: describe a sun through temperature, radius, mass, radiant
        output and activity. Use coherent stellar families to choose these
        together, then compress their extremes for play. Spectral class is an
        identity, not a difficulty rating. The encounter comparisons below
        remain proposals; they are not measured flight results or accepted
        tuning.
      </p>
      <table>
        <caption>
          From stellar reference to playable consequence
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Real characteristic</th>
            <th>Proposed model and fiction</th>
            <th>What the pilot reads</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Temperature → colour.</strong>
              O–B stars are hotter and bluer; K–M cooler and redder. These
              classes describe a temperature sequence.
              <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/stars/"
                >NASA: stellar classes</a
              >
            </td>
            <td>
              Keep the ordering. A filtered flight display exaggerates colour
              differences and limits glare; saturated neon is a display
              convention.
            </td>
            <td>
              Colour identifies the family immediately. It does not alone
              promise safety or tell the strength of the pull.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Radius and luminosity.</strong>
              Total radiant output depends on both emitting area and
              temperature: L ∝ R²T⁴. A cool, expanded giant can still be
              luminous.
              <a href="https://spacemath.gsfc.nasa.gov/SMBooks/SMBook5.pdf"
                >NASA: radiation model</a
              >
            </td>
            <td>
              Keep radius separate from output. Compress sizes and exposure
              distances. Fictional shielding allows a short encounter; it does
              not make the luminous body traversable.
            </td>
            <td>
              The disc occupies space; exposure increases on approach. A larger
              disc does not automatically mean a stronger gravitational well.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Mass and distance → gravity.</strong>
              Outside a spherical star, gravitational acceleration follows mass
              divided by distance squared.
              <a
                href="https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/weight-gravitational-force/"
                >NASA: gravity</a
              >
            </td>
            <td>
              Preserve attraction and increasing pull inward. Compress strength
              and retain a bounded arena reach as a gameplay abstraction, not a
              physical edge.
            </td>
            <td>
              The trajectory bends before contact. An inward dive gains speed
              but creates an outward climb; gravity alone is not a free
              permanent speed boost.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>Activity is a separate trait.</strong>
              Magnetic energy drives solar flares; red dwarfs can also produce
              powerful eruptions.
              <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/solar-storms-and-flares/"
                >NASA: solar activity</a
              >;
              <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/exoplanets/neighboring-stars-bad-behavior-large-and-frequent-flares/"
                >Proxima’s flares</a
              >
            </td>
            <td>
              Begin with steady stars. If activity earns a mechanic, use a
              legible build-up and bounded event. Reliable advance warning would
              be a fictional sensor capability.
            </td>
            <td>
              Steady means predictable, not harmless. An active star adds timing
              decisions independently of its colour.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>A luminous atmosphere.</strong>
              The Sun has a visible photosphere and an extended corona; it has
              no solid surface.
              <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/">NASA: the Sun</a>
            </td>
            <td>
              Use a crisp luminous body as the terminal boundary and a
              restrained atmosphere for presence. Exposure is an abstraction of
              the ship’s response, not the gas temperature at each point.
            </td>
            <td>
              Contact reads as destruction. A decorative glow must not
              masquerade as an exact safe-distance marker.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3 id="stellar-decisions">02.1 decisions / The standard sun model</h3>
      <p>
        The stellar model above is the foundation for further exploration and
        the eventual Suns reference article. Temperature, radius, mass, radiant
        output and activity have distinct meanings. Real stellar relationships
        guide coherent combinations; the game’s compressed representation does
        not require colour, gravity reach or charging reach to be calculated
        directly from the visible disc radius.
      </p>
      <ul class="decisions">
        <li>
          <strong>Baseline:</strong>
          begin with our own Sun as the familiar reference, represented as a
          steady Sun-like star. Establish this encounter before varying the
          stellar family.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gravity:</strong>
          size the field in relation to its maximum pull, with a finite cutoff.
          The visible body does not alone determine its reach. This fixes a
          design relationship, not new tuning values or a revised force law.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Solar charging:</strong>
          treat its strength, distance profile and outer cutoff as an unresolved
          design. Neither the body radius nor the gravity boundary automatically
          supplies the charging boundary.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scope:</strong>
          regular stars, from small to large. Exotic objects such as quasars and
          fictional miniature black holes remain future subjects. The baseline
          does not settle the eventual stellar catalogue or require an eruption
          mechanic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Documentation:</strong>
          retain the scientific reasoning, fictional simplifications and their
          gameplay meanings together. The three encounter sketches remain
          hypotheses until tried in play.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The next investigation is the Sun-like charging field. Compare how a
        pilot gains energy across distance, where collection stops, and how that
        field overlaps gravity and damaging exposure. A linear ramp is one
        candidate, not an adopted law. Percentage examples such as 10–90% or
        10–100% remain illustrative until their distance interval and reference
        charging rate are explicitly defined. Luminosity describes the source;
        usable charging also depends on the collector and its limits.
      </p>
      <h3>Three encounters to distinguish first</h3>
      <p>
        Start with main-sequence suns: cool K/M, solar-like G/F and hot A/B/O.
        Keep the seven class names available, but first establish three readable
        experiences. Their relative size, mass and output follow the broad
        main-sequence trend; all three examples below are steady. Evolved giants
        and compact remnants would need their own families, not another step on
        this ladder.
        <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/types/"
          >NASA: stellar families</a
        >.
      </p>
      <div class="encounters">
        <article>
          <h4>Cool / intimate</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>A compact, warm-coloured presence.</strong>
            Lower output and mass in this proposed main-sequence family.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Approach:</strong>
              travel closer before exposure matters.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pass:</strong>
              work near a small body; modest energy income makes lingering
              tempting.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Leave:</strong>
              a gentler pull gives more room to correct, provided the pilot has
              not skimmed the fatal boundary.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Desired feel: precise and approachable. An active M variant could
            overturn the calm without changing its identity.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>Solar-like / deliberate</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>The reference encounter.</strong>
            Intermediate body, pull and radiant output.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Approach:</strong>
              recognise the bend in the path and plan the exit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pass:</strong>
              a sustained arc offers useful collection, while exposure makes
              staying costly.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Leave:</strong>
              commit thrust while there is still room to climb out.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Desired feel: a learnable bargain. This is where we establish what
            fair warning and useful reward mean.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h4>Hot / commanding</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>A large, pale blue-white presence.</strong>
            Higher output and mass, compressed to fit the arena.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Approach:</strong>
              react farther out; both exposure and trajectory demand attention.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pass:</strong>
              make a wide, committed sweep. High local collection rate is
              countered by rapid exposure cost.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Leave:</strong>
              begin the exit earlier; a late inward correction should feel
              consequential.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Desired feel: commanding and expensive to misjudge. Greater
            luminosity does not automatically guarantee a better net energy
            bargain.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        Scenario conditions: the same assembled ship, collection equipment,
        part-filled reserves and undamaged hull; no planets, debris or random
        eruptions. These are authored encounter sequences, not simulated paths.
        They expose the intended choices for discussion; escape margins and
        energy profit still require trials with the production Motion
        instrument.
      </p>
      <h3>What the fiction needs to carry</h3>
      <p>
        Proposed allowance: ships have enough protection to operate briefly in
        stellar proximity, and compact collectors convert incident stellar
        energy into usable power. Keep collection distinct from fictional
        gravity-coil recovery. A bare ship should still encounter the same
        radiation and gravity, without inheriting a collector’s benefit. Use
        existing hull loss to express damaging exposure initially; a new heat
        reserve or cooling minigame is not yet justified.
      </p>
      <p>
        Distances, ship size, stellar size and encounter duration use
        deliberately compressed scales. Stars remain stable over a run; stellar
        evolution, internal fusion and detailed spectra inform identity without
        becoming simulations. A luminous body, restrained atmospheric motion and
        an exposure cue can carry the fiction. Reserve expanding warning pulses
        for meaningful events if events are adopted, rather than teaching that
        every star continually emits dangerous circular waves.
      </p>
      <h3>Implications to investigate next</h3>
      <p>
        The current configuration couples radius, mass, power, burn and pulse
        speed along O–M. The proposed model separates their meanings before
        retuning any values. Flight feedback should distinguish
        <em>collection</em>
        from <em>net power change</em> and show damaging exposure where the
        pilot already reads hull status. A collection boundary depends on
        equipment; it cannot also promise safety or escape. Keep decorative
        corona, damage threshold and gravity reach conceptually distinct.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agreed sequence: establish the steady Sun-like encounter first, then
        test the cool and hot ends against it. Leave eruptions and evolved stars
        as extensions until the basic pass offers a worthwhile decision.
        Unsettled: whether collection is worth approaching for with the actual
        loadouts, how early damage must become legible, and whether three felt
        families give the seven class labels enough meaning.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>02.2 / Collecting inside the well</summary>
      <h3 id="charging-field">02.2 / Collecting inside the well</h3>
      <p>
        First hypothesis: for Terra’s Sun, the outer solar-collection boundary
        coincides with the gravity boundary. Crossing inward begins both
        collection and attraction. Energy recovery therefore asks the pilot to
        enter the well. This is a trial premise, not a settled field law or a
        claim that every point inside the well is equally dangerous.
      </p>
      <figure class="field-sketch">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 1040 400"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="field-title field-desc"
        >
          <title id="field-title">
            Three proposed relationships between collection reach and gravity
            reach
          </title>
          <desc id="field-desc">
            Distance increases to the right from a fixed stellar surface.
            Gravity ends at the same dashed line in all three examples. Terra’s
            solar collection ends there too; the generous example extends beyond
            it, and the demanding example ends inside it. These are schematic
            field extents, not force or charging curves.
          </desc>
          <text x="240" y="26">Stellar surface</text>
          <text x="760" y="26">Distance outward →</text>
          <line x1="240" y1="40" x2="240" y2="350" class="surface" />
          <line x1="760" y1="40" x2="760" y2="350" class="boundary" />
          <text x="765" y="380">Gravity cutoff</text>
          <text x="0" y="83">Terra / shared reach</text>
          <path d="M240 72 H760" class="gravity" />
          <path d="M240 100 H760" class="collection" />
          <text x="0" y="193">Extended collection</text>
          <path d="M240 182 H760" class="gravity" />
          <path d="M240 210 H970" class="collection" />
          <text x="0" y="303">Deep collection</text>
          <path d="M240 292 H760" class="gravity" />
          <path d="M240 320 H500" class="collection" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Schematic, not to scale. Solid pale line: gravity reach. Dashed blue
          line: collection reach. Identical body and gravity field in all three
          examples isolate the effect of moving only the collection boundary.
          Line length says where an effect exists, not how strong it is.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The shared boundary gives the baseline a simple promise: collecting
        stellar energy exposes the ship to gravitational pull. Extending
        collection beyond it could permit recovery without that pull;
        restricting collection to the interior could demand a deeper commitment.
        These are relative gravitational risks. Neither an outer collection band
        nor a weak pull guarantees safety from radiation, contact or other
        hazards.
      </p>
      <h3>Collection begins before the battery necessarily recovers</h3>
      <p>
        Keep three thresholds distinct: the outer edge where the collector
        begins supplying power; the distance where supply exceeds this ship’s
        current consumption; and the exposure at which hull damage begins. Only
        the first is proposed to coincide with Terra’s gravity boundary. A ship
        can be collecting while its battery is still draining. Thrust, equipment
        and collector capacity move the break-even point.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the next curve comparison, a linear rise from zero at the outer edge
        is a useful starting candidate: entry is gentle and the pilot must
        travel inward for useful income. Starting at a nonzero rate would give
        an immediate reward but create a step at the boundary. Neither is chosen
        yet. Peak rate, its distance from the surface and the damage profile
        remain open. A narrow field alone does not establish an extremely
        dangerous sun; useful net recovery must actually require a consequential
        inward commitment.
      </p>
      <h3>Compact radiant-energy collection</h3>
      <p>
        The fictional direction is a compact collector integrated with the ship,
        without large deployable solar panels. It harvests stellar radiation
        through advanced collection technology; the exact mechanism remains
        undefined. The finite charging field represents the useful operating
        envelope of that technology, not a place where sunlight physically ends.
        Solar collection remains distinct from gravity-based energy recovery.
      </p>
      <p>
        This sketch establishes an inspectable spatial hypothesis only. It does
        not establish charging rates, profitable flight paths or escape margins.
        The next comparison should hold the Sun, ship and gravity field fixed
        while varying the collection profile, showing gross collection and net
        power separately before testing the pass in flight.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>
        02.3 / Linear charging and the selected 7.5 E/s reference
      </summary>
      <h3 id="charging-curves">
        02.3 / A linear collection curve on real flight paths
      </h3>
      <p>
        This develops 02.2’s shared-boundary hypothesis. Collection rises
        linearly from zero at the gravity edge to a candidate maximum at the
        surface: C(d) = peak × (reach − d) / (reach − radius), clamped to zero
        outside. The original 4 E/s peak used the current G-star inner-zone rate
        for one collector, replacing only the stepped profile in this study’s
        energy calculation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Wasp is the reference ship for these trials: one Light ion engine,
        stabilizers enabled, empty equipment slots and inherent collection
        equivalent to one solar collector. No solar fitting is required. Use the
        controls inside the path instrument to compare coasting and powered
        consumption across three approach offsets using the shared Motion
        instrument. B1, B2 and B3 change the flight program; the time cursor
        connects the paths to the four energy graphs. The starting offsets are
        experimental conditions; the burn inputs generate these paths through
        the Wasp’s production motion rules. The paths make the game’s behaviour
        inspectable rather than prescribing its outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        The energy-budget controls vary life-support draw, ion power draw and
        peak solar collection over the same Wasp paths. The linear profile and
        shared field boundary stay fixed. Ion draw changes energy costs only,
        including stabilizer costs; thrust and handling stay unchanged. Battery
        change starts at zero, revealing whether a whole manoeuvre recovers more
        than it spends. Reset restores the selected experiment budget without
        changing the selected burn.
      </p>
      <h3 id="charging-selection">Selected standard-Sun reference / 7.5 E/s</h3>
      <p>
        Adopted Path tool requirement: energy-collection fields belong in path
        analysis alongside gravity fields. The shared instrument must support
        gross collection, consumption, net charging and cumulative battery
        change, synchronized with the ship’s path and time cursor. Collection
        reach and profile are independently defined from gravity. This study
        demonstrates the readings; reusable collection-field configuration
        remains delivery work beyond the study-local calculation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Human review found the 7.5 E/s peak satisfying across different burns
        and Wasp paths. This is the selected maximum collection rate for the
        standard Sun, per inherent collector-equivalent. Retain it as the
        reference for subsequent field-shape exploration and later comparisons
        with other stars. The retained experiment opens at 0.8 E/s life-support
        draw and 1.0 E/s Light ion draw. These consumption values are experiment
        settings, not adopted changes to every ship’s production rules.
      </p>
      <p>
        The successful result establishes the energy-budget direction. The next
        hypothesis is to retain the 7.5 E/s maximum at the surface or slightly
        outside it, falling to 10% (0.75 E/s) at the field edge. The peak’s
        position, inner plateau and outer cutoff remain unsettled. The
        instrument below preserves the tested linear-to-zero curve; it does not
        silently substitute that next hypothesis. Capacity caps and damaging
        exposure still need assessment before implementation.
      </p>
      <ChargingStudy client:load />
      <p>
        The existing instrument lacked energy traces. This requested comparison
        adds collection, consumption and signed net charging to its shared
        graphs. The linear solar law stays study-local. Motion uses production
        propulsion and the current gravity tuning; no gravity constants are
        changed. Consumption is sampled from production buildDrain at each
        recorded flight state. The original 4 E/s peak with 1.5 E/s life support
        and 2 E/s ion draw yielded coasting break-even at about 216 u and
        forward-thrust break-even at about 71 u from the centre (surface 35 u;
        reach 324 u). Under that original budget, the B1 passes all spent more
        energy than they collected across the full 14 seconds, despite brief
        recovery on the closer paths. These historical results describe the
        initial budget, not the selected 7.5 E/s reference. Net energy totals
        use these 60 Hz samples and exclude battery saturation, power starvation
        and radiation damage. They establish the candidate energy budget, not
        survival or the final integrated flight experience.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>02.4 / Accepted collection-field shapes</summary>
      <h3 id="field-shape">02.4 / A useful edge and an accessible peak</h3>
      <p>
        Preserve the selected 7.5 E/s peak, 0.8 E/s life support and 1.0 E/s ion
        draw while comparing three collection profiles on the same simulated
        Wasp paths. Open the hamburger pane to switch between the retained
        reference, a 10% outer edge, and that same edge with a 20 u inner
        shoulder. The shoulder distance is an exploratory fixture, not an
        accepted value.
      </p>
      <p>
        At this budget the 10% edge supplies 0.75 E/s: just below the 0.8 E/s
        life-support cost. Entry nearly pays for coasting, but forward thrust
        still requires moving inward. The shoulder makes maximum collection
        accessible outside the luminous surface; it does not establish a safe
        place to linger. Damage and ship contact clearance still constrain
        access.
      </p>
      <p>
        A nonzero edge also creates a discontinuity: collection jumps between
        0.75 E/s at the boundary and zero immediately outside it. Look for that
        step in the collection trace, and compare how early net charging starts
        and how much the battery recovers. Switching profiles preserves the
        burn, starting positions and paths; these are field comparisons, not new
        flight scenarios. The previous instrument remains an independent
        retained reference.
      </p>

      <h3>Accepted charging profiles</h3>
      <p>
        Both the 10% edge and the 10% edge with a 20 u shoulder are accepted as
        useful standard-Sun charging profiles. Preserve both exact variants: 7.5
        E/s maximum per collector-equivalent, 0.75 E/s at the shared outer
        gravity/collection boundary, zero outside; the shoulder variant
        maintains the maximum from the surface to 20 u beyond it. The reference
        experiment uses Wasp, 0.8 E/s life support and 1.0 E/s ion draw.
        Acceptance concerns the observed charging behaviour, not yet radiation
        balance or one chosen production default. Both variants remain available
        in the instrument.
      </p>
      <ChargingStudy
        client:load
        compareShapes
        label="Sun field-shape comparison"
      />
    </details>
    <h3 id="exposure-economy">02.5 / Trading hull for energy</h3>
    <p>
      The damage envelope should be much smaller than the collection field. This
      round compares local energy income and hull cost directly across distance,
      without flight paths. Two deliberately simple candidate damage envelopes
      test how much high-income space can remain free of radiation damage.
      Repair is optional fitted equipment; its assumed 0.2 E/s running cost is
      included conditionally, while its restoration rate remains open.
    </p>
    <p>
      Damage has its own reach, intensity and profile, independently specified
      from collection and gravity. Collection represents harvestable stellar
      radiation; damage represents exposure that exceeds the ship’s protection.
      This is a gameplay distinction, not a claim that light is separate from
      energy. Stellar colour is a proposed readable clue to hazardous radiation;
      atmospheric appearance and flare behaviour may communicate activity. The
      mapping from those cues to damage reach and severity is still to be
      explored. A large collection field must not automatically imply a large
      damage envelope, and permanent exposure must remain distinguishable from
      an intermittent flare.
    </p>
    <p>
      Settled placement: maximum damage begins at the shoulder’s outer edge, 20
      u beyond the surface, and remains at maximum inward to the surface. The
      comparison uses 1.5 hull/s as its provisional maximum; the outer damage
      boundaries and linear-versus-stepped decline remain open.
    </p>
    <ExposureEconomy />

    <h3 id="sun-hud-preview">02.6 / Field states in the game view</h3>
    <p>
      A presentation trial using the production G-star animation and Wasp
      renderer. The side switches expose the approved Suns HUD states directly.
      All boundary strokes are one CSS pixel, drawn after world projection;
      camera zoom changes the circle’s apparent radius, never its stroke width.
      The existing Sun animation is retained, not redesigned.
    </p>
    <SunHudPreview client:load />
    <p>
      Optional fill toggles compare barely visible 3.3% Android-green solar and
      11% red hazard interiors. Each fill appears only while its corresponding
      field is visible and active; outlines retain their existing treatment.
      Both fills are approved for the standard-Sun HUD.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android green is the common field language. Gravity is dashed; solar
      availability uses a solid boundary, brightened when collection is active.
      At their shared radius, the solar line is drawn 2 CSS pixels inside the
      gravity line so both remain readable. This visual offset stays fixed at
      every zoom and does not change either gameplay boundary. Hazard is muted
      amber until damage is active, then red. Text repeats active states so
      colour is not the only cue. Active collection does not imply net charging.
      These are approved presentation states; toggles force the states and
      relocate the ship for context. No production HUD behaviour has changed.
    </p>
    <h3 id="standard-sun-decision">Delivery decision / One standard Sun</h3>
    <p>
      This study now delivers the standard Sun only. Its mechanics and geometry
      are settled; remaining presentation work is limited to HUD information and
      field lines, including their states in relevant situations. Other stellar
      types are reserved for a confirmed follow-up study. Earlier comparisons
      remain evidence, not additional delivery scope.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>
        Locked standard-Sun mechanics
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Property</th>
          <th>Selected value</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Star identity and surface</td>
          <td>G / standard Sun; 35 u radius</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shoulder</td>
          <td>20 u beyond the surface; outer radius 55 u</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Collection</td>
          <td>
            7.5 E/s per collector-equivalent through the shoulder; linear
            decline to 0.75 E/s at the outer edge; zero outside
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Collection reach</td>
          <td>
            Same as the unchanged standard gravity field: approximately 324 u
            from centre
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Damage</td>
          <td>
            1.5 hull/s through the shoulder; linear decline to zero at 100 u
            from centre; zero beyond
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Life-support draw</td>
          <td>0.8 E/s</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Light ion draw</td>
          <td>1.0 E/s; energy cost only, with thrust and handling unchanged</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Inherent collection</td>
          <td>
            One solar collector-equivalent on every ship; fittings add to it
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reference ship</td>
          <td>Wasp</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      All distances above are centre-based except the explicitly stated shoulder
      width. The 100 u damage boundary is not another 100 u beyond the shoulder.
      Gravity tuning is retained. Production battery capacity, depletion and
      contact remain real constraints. Optional repair’s 0.2 E/s was a
      comparison assumption; repair output and implementation are not part of
      this commitment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scope includes making current game sun presentations consistently
      standard, shared mechanic definitions, the Path tool’s reusable
      energy-field analysis, and a polished Suns Lab article explaining fiction,
      zones and expected in-game behaviour. Colour and activity mappings for
      other stars belong in the confirmed stellar-variety follow-up, after this
      standard is delivered.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / Standard Sun is ready for integration</h2>
    <p>
      The standard-Sun mechanics and HUD model are settled and ready for a
      bounded implementation. No further visual exploration blocks this patch.
      The production game already supplies inherent collection, fitted-collector
      scaling, resource capacity, propulsion and gravity. The study uses their
      motion and consumption definitions and adds the accepted profiles as an
      energy calculation over the paths.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production solar charging still uses three stepped zones scaled from the
      sun’s radius, in both assembled and stock resource paths. Delivery needs
      one shared collection-field definition with explicit reach, peak, outer
      fraction and shoulder, consumed by play and the Path tool. The
      standard-Sun-only delivery adopts the locked 0.8 E/s life-support and 1.0
      E/s Light ion costs, preserving thrust and handling; other engine families
      are outside this change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Battery saturation and depletion already affect production flight, but are
      deliberately absent from the retained experiment. An integrated trial must
      show actual stored energy, wasted collection at capacity, and propulsion
      when reserves are depleted. The shipping profile is now the 10% edge with
      a 20 u shoulder and the 100 u damage envelope. Retained alternatives
      remain study evidence.
    </p>
    <h3>Integration and HUD / field-line feedback</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Every ship gets one inherent solar collector-equivalent.</strong>
      No fitting, equipment slot or unlock is required. Fitted collectors add to
      that baseline. The existing assembled-loadout calculation already starts
      at one; integration must preserve this and verify the same rule for every
      stock, assembled and unfitted ship path. Wasp remains the reference ship,
      not the only ship eligible to collect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current hull damage also uses radius-scaled zones. For the current G-star
      radius of 35 u, their outer boundaries are 87.5, 148.75 and 210 u; the
      proposed shoulder ends at 55 u from the centre. It therefore lies within
      the current innermost damage band. This is a code-derived overlap, not a
      finding that the shoulder is too dangerous. Exploration 02.5 established
      the selected trade through distance-based energy and hull rates. The new
      damage profile is settled in the delivery decision above. Implementation
      must verify its interaction with actual battery limits and contact.
      Optional repair output remains outside scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current simulation creates a G star, but the decorative landing system
      still selects from multiple stellar types. Standard-Sun delivery must
      align that presentation too. Technical reuse is strong; no new physics
      system is needed. Remaining delivery work includes shared field
      configuration, integrated resource feedback, world/HUD cues and the Suns
      article. The animated preview establishes the approved presentation, not
      live gameplay behaviour. Integration still needs in-game verification;
      readiness does not claim production implementation is complete.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>04 / A bounded standard-Sun patch</h2>
    <h3>Settled Suns HUD constraints</h3>
    <p>
      Android green, from the shared Android-green token, is the primary colour
      for Suns HUD lines. Supporting colours may distinguish hazard states. This
      is scoped to Suns; it does not redefine the game’s brand or other HUDs.
      Field boundaries remain world-anchored, but every HUD stroke is one CSS
      pixel wide at every camera zoom and supported display resolution. Device
      pixel ratio affects rasterization, not the intended visible stroke width.
      Active states must not thicken the line.
    </p>
    <p>
      The approved model uses dim Android-green dashed gravity and solid solar
      boundaries; active collection brightens the solar line and adds a 3.3%
      Android-green fill. Hazard is dotted in both states: muted amber when
      inactive, red with an 11% red fill when damage is active. Fills are absent
      in inactive states. The preview toggles remain comparison aids; production
      derives activation from actual ship effects. Collection may be active
      while net battery change is negative; active hazard means damage is being
      applied. At coincident gravity/solar boundaries, draw solar 2 CSS pixels
      inside gravity while both are visible. This fixed screen-space offset does
      not change either gameplay reach. All strokes remain 1 CSS pixel wide.
    </p>

    <p>
      Deliver the settled mechanics now, with presentation limited to HUD
      information, field lines and their states. Sun artwork, palette changes,
      corona redesign and other effects are deferred and are not prerequisites.
      Existing usable rendering stays the starting point. No change to the
      standing experience-design principles is proposed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Integration updates the owning star, survival, propulsion and game-screen
      specs alongside their behaviour, plus the Motion instrument spec for Path
      energy analysis. The existing star spec describes an older implementation
      and must be reconciled against current code and these decisions. The Lab
      article documents the implemented standard, including fiction, inherent
      collection, zone boundaries, battery limits and the HUD’s visual inset.
    </p>
